The recent discussion of nymph gender roles, charisma/sexual attraction,
etc. has provoked some thought.

I was thinking, well the game is oriented towards men because mostly men
play it.  However we want the game to be realistic. How many women
actually would do something like this?  Well, let's take a look.
I've never heard of a woman knight (maybe Joan of Arc is an exception).
Woman fighters, barabarians, and archeologists are certainly not unheardof,
but usually they are men.  Additionally, the game seems to be set
in a fantasy medieval type realm.  Thus it seems that the game should
make sense, not in light of our modern ideas about gender roles, but
in terms of the medieval mind set and the mythology on which the
game is based.

I suggest that the devo team take each character type and consider 
whether it is realistic to have male or females of that type.
Fighters might always be men.  Valkaries might always be women (in
fact, they should always be, I would think).   Wizards could go
either way, as could healers and probably tourists.  

You get the idea.  This was suggested to me by the arcade game, Gauntlet,
in which the Valkary is clearly a woman and the fighter, elf, and wizard
are clearly men.  My suggestion is that nethack should be realistic
and that worrying about its being sexist should not encroach on its
realism.  If sexism harms the realism then clean it up.  By realism,
I don't mean stereotypes, I mean the way things are or are thought of
as being (in the case of purely mythological creatures.)  


Well, that's my lengthy 2 cents worth.

